WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.880
Updating application images does not patch the platform beneath them.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:09.060
Kubernetes nodes have operating systems, kernels, container runtimes, agents, drivers,

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:13.840
and management components with separate vulnerabilities and release cycles.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:19.100
Control-plane components and cluster add-ons also require inventory and maintenance.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.960
Node updates often require cordoning, draining, replacement, or restart.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:28.500
Workloads must tolerate rescheduling through replicas, readiness checks,

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:33.120
graceful shutdown, storage design, and realistic resource capacity.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:37.420
A patched node is not useful if maintenance causes an avoidable service outage.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:40.840
Rolling updates replace workload instances gradually.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:46.020
Deployment settings control surge, unavailable replicas, and health evaluation.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:50.400
Pod disruption budgets can protect minimum availability during voluntary disruption,

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:53.660
but an unrealistic budget can also block node maintenance.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.720
Cluster compatibility matters.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:01.540
Kubernetes versions, runtimes, network and storage plugins, admission systems,

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.360
and application APIs must remain within supported combinations.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:08.940
Review version-skew policy, deprecations, vendor guidance,

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:12.360
and rollback constraints before updating platform components.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:14.980
Coordinate application and platform teams.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.400
Application owners understand workload health and data behavior;

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:24.140
platform teams manage nodes, scheduling, runtimes, and control-plane dependencies.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:29.320
Security provides prioritization and verifies that exposed versions were actually removed.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:31.060
For managed services,

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:36.160
clarify which layers the provider patches and which remain the customer's responsibility.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:41.100
Test maintenance in representative environments, monitor workloads and cluster health,

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:46.440
retain recovery procedures, and verify node and component inventory after rollout.
